<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <meta name="description" content="Meet the Patch Wizards: five computer science master's students exploring secure, human-guided AI software engineering at Paderborn University.">
  <title>Team Patch Wizards | Agentic SE Lab</title>
  <script>
    (function () {
      try {
        const saved = localStorage.getItem("upb-theme");
        const theme = saved === "light" || saved === "dark"
          ? saved
          : (window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches ? "dark" : "light");
        document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", theme);
      } catch (_) {
        document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "light");
      }
    }());
  </script>
  <style>
    :root {
      color-scheme: light;
      --bg: #FFF8E7;
      --text: #1D1D1F;
      --secondary: #5A5A5F;
      --card: #FFFFFF;
      --accent: #B54732;
      --border: #E8DCC3;
      --shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08);
      --header-glow: rgba(181, 71, 50, 0.10);
    }

    html[data-theme="dark"] {
      color-scheme: dark;
      --bg: #14142B;
      --text: #F4F1DE;
      --secondary: #B8B5A7;
      --card: #1F1F3D;
      --accent: #D67960;
      --border: #39395F;
      --shadow: none;
      --header-glow: rgba(214, 121, 96, 0.10);
    }

    html {
      scroll-behavior: smooth;
      scroll-padding-top: 2rem;
    }

    body {
      margin: 0;
      background: var(--bg);
      color: var(--text);
      font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
      font-size: 1rem;
      line-height: 1.6;
      transition: background-color 180ms ease, color 180ms ease;
    }

    button,
    a {
      font: inherit;
    }

    a {
      color: var(--accent);
      text-underline-offset: 0.25rem;
    }

    a:hover {
      text-decoration-thickness: 0.15rem;
    }

    .site-header {
      background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--bg), var(--header-glow));
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    }

    .header-inner,
    main {
      width: min(70rem, calc(100% - 3rem));
      margin: 0 auto;
    }

    .header-inner {
      position: relative;
      padding: 3rem 0 2rem;
    }

    h1,
    h2,
    h3,
    p {
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    h1 {
      max-width: 14ch;
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
      font-size: 2.5rem;
      line-height: 1.1;
      letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    }

    h2 {
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
      font-size: 1.75rem;
      line-height: 1.2;
      letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    }

    h3 {
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
      font-size: 1.25rem;
      line-height: 1.3;
    }

    .tagline {
      max-width: 60ch;
      margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
      color: var(--secondary);
      font-size: 1.1rem;
    }

    .theme-toggle {
      position: absolute;
      top: 2rem;
      right: 0;
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 999px;
      background: var(--card);
      color: var(--text);
      box-shadow: var(--shadow);
      cursor: pointer;
    }

    .theme-toggle:hover {
      border-color: var(--accent);
    }

    .theme-toggle:focus-visible,
    a:focus-visible {
      outline: 0.2rem solid var(--accent);
      outline-offset: 0.25rem;
    }

    nav {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 1.5rem;
    }

    nav a {
      font-weight: 650;
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    main {
      padding-bottom: 3rem;
    }

    section {
      margin-top: 3rem;
    }

    .intro {
      max-width: 68ch;
      color: var(--secondary);
    }

    .intro p {
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }

    .team-note {
      display: inline-block;
      margin-top: 0.5rem;
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      border-left: 0.25rem solid var(--accent);
      background: var(--card);
      color: var(--text);
      box-shadow: var(--shadow);
    }

    .member-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
      gap: 1.5rem;
      margin-top: 2rem;
    }

    .member-card {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: flex-start;
      padding: 1.5rem;
      border-radius: 1rem;
      background: var(--card);
      box-shadow: var(--shadow);
    }

    .member-card img {
      width: 8rem;
      height: 8rem;
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
      border-radius: 50%;
      object-fit: cover;
    }

    html[data-theme="dark"] .member-card img {
      filter: brightness(0.9);
    }

    .study {
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
      color: var(--secondary);
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      font-weight: 650;
    }

    .bio {
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }

    .fun-fact {
      margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
      color: var(--secondary);
    }

    .hot-take {
      margin-top: auto;
      margin-bottom: 0;
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      border-radius: 0.5rem;
      background: var(--accent);
      color: #FFFFFF;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .future-panel {
      padding: 2rem;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 1rem;
      background: var(--card);
      box-shadow: var(--shadow);
    }

    .future-panel > p {
      max-width: 70ch;
      color: var(--secondary);
    }

    .principles {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 1rem 2rem;
      margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;
      padding: 0;
      list-style: none;
    }

    .principles li {
      padding-left: 1.5rem;
      position: relative;
    }

    .principles li::before {
      position: absolute;
      left: 0;
      color: var(--accent);
      content: "✦";
    }

    .disagreement {
      padding: 1.5rem;
      border-radius: 0.75rem;
      background: var(--bg);
    }

    .disagreement h3 {
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }

    .disagreement p {
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }

    .disagreement p:last-child {
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }

    footer {
      padding: 2rem 1rem;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
      color: var(--secondary);
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      text-align: center;
    }

    footer p {
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }

    @media (max-width: 700px) {
      .header-inner,
      main {
        width: calc(100% - 2rem);
      }

      .header-inner {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        padding: 2rem 0;
      }

      h1 {
        padding-right: 0;
      }

      .theme-toggle {
        position: static;
        order: 0;
        align-self: flex-end;
        margin-bottom: 1rem;
      }

      .header-inner h1 {
        order: 1;
      }

      .tagline {
        order: 2;
      }

      nav {
        order: 3;
      }

      .member-grid,
      .principles {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }

      .future-panel {
        padding: 1.5rem;
      }
    }

    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      html {
        scroll-behavior: auto;
      }

      *,
      *::before,
      *::after {
        transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
        animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
        animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
      }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="header-inner">
      <button id="theme-toggle" class="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode">
        <span aria-hidden="true">🌙</span> <span class="toggle-label">Night mode</span>
      </button>
      <h1>Patch Wizards</h1>
      <p class="tagline">Five humans, a suspicious number of security questions, and absolutely no magic fix for production.</p>
      <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href="#team">Meet the team</a>
        <a href="#future-of-se">Our AI outlook</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section id="team" aria-labelledby="team-heading">
      <div class="intro">
        <h2 id="team-heading">Meet the Patch Wizards</h2>
        <p>We are five computer science master's students at Paderborn University, bringing together curiosity about AI, software, and the security holes that tend to appear when nobody is looking. In the Agentic Software Engineering Lab, we explore how to build useful systems without letting confidence outrun correctness.</p>
        <p>Our shared motto: automate the boring bits, then inspect the interesting bits twice.</p>
        <p class="team-note"><strong>Current team status:</strong> curious, caffeinated, and reviewing the diff.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="member-grid">
        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:Jenith_avatar.png}}" alt="Avatar of Jenith">
          <h3>Jenith</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, Paderborn University</p>
          <p class="bio">Jenith studies computer science at Paderborn University and is drawn to cybersecurity, SOC operations, and vulnerability assessment and penetration testing. Python, network systems, and puzzling out why traffic has stopped moving are also firmly in the toolkit. AI can speed up code, tests, and documentation work, but Jenith warns that it can miss system-wide context and quietly introduce logic flaws or security vulnerabilities. Every generated line still needs a human gatekeeper.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: A misplaced router rule once turned a network script into a digital traffic jam.</em></p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI won't replace human minds; it can provide peace of mind—if you know how to use it.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:prachi_avatar.png}}" alt="Avatar of Prachi">
          <h3>Prachi</h3>
          <p class="study">Master's in Computer Science, Paderborn University</p>
          <p class="bio">Prachi studies computer science at Paderborn University and enjoys debugging, security operations, and travel. Small AI-assisted jobs such as simple code and tests can save time, but code that looks right can still be wrong—and that distinction matters especially in security work. Prachi sees software engineers writing less code by hand over the next several years, while spending more time checking and testing generated work. Design, safety, and finding bugs will remain human-shaped responsibilities.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Prachi is 23, not 15 years old!</em></p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI will not take your job. But someone who can check AI code well might.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:pratham_avatar.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Pratham">
          <h3>Pratham</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, Paderborn University</p>
          <p class="bio">Pratham has a bachelor's degree in computer science from the University of Mumbai and is now pursuing a master's at Paderborn University. Cybersecurity, AI, and anime share the interest list; DIY audio and custom home-theater builds round it out. Pratham sees AI as a useful boilerplate-and-repetition remover, but one that can gloss over critical security details. A real understanding of network protocols and hardware interfaces is still needed to make sure generated code is secure and actually works.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Pratham builds custom home-theater systems for fun.</em></p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI will write 90% of our code, and we will spend 100% of our time patching the security vulnerabilities it introduces.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:vaibhav_avatar.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Vaibhav">
          <h3>Vaibhav</h3>
          <p class="study">Master's in Computer Science, Paderborn University</p>
          <p class="bio">Vaibhav studies computer science at Paderborn University and is interested in artificial intelligence, anime, and philosophy. A huge fan of the Harry Potter movies, Vaibhav is also thinking about how dramatically AI is changing software development: it can reduce the time spent writing code and fixing bugs. Looking five to ten years ahead, Vaibhav imagines AI taking on nearly the whole journey from raw idea to working software. The rest of the team is keeping a review window open.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Vaibhav is a huge fan of the Harry Potter movies.</em></p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI makes shipping software faster, but it trades deep critical thinking for security bugs.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:vatsal_avatar.jpeg}}" alt="Photo of Vatsal">
          <h3>Vatsal</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, Paderborn University; B.Eng. Computer Engineering, CHARUSAT University</p>
          <p class="bio">Vatsal completed a bachelor's in computer engineering at CHARUSAT University and is pursuing a master's in computer science at Paderborn University. Interests include AI, anime, volleyball, sightseeing, and dramas. Vatsal sees AI helping developers turn ideas into software faster and expects it to assist at every stage of engineering in the future. That promise comes with a serious trust question: generated systems still need to be checked for reliability and security before anyone calls them ready.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Vatsal may be reserved at first, but is open with friends.</em></p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI can make routine tasks easy; trusting its code without checking security is the real challenge.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-heading">
      <h2 id="future-heading">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
      <div class="future-panel">
        <p>We don't all predict the same destination, but we agree on the things that will matter on the way there. Our shared view is less “robots take over” and more “the review queue is about to get interesting.”</p>
        <ul class="principles">
          <li>AI is already useful for boilerplate, repetitive work, first drafts, and getting from idea to prototype faster.</li>
          <li>Generated code can be plausible and still be wrong. Human review, tests, and system-level context remain essential.</li>
          <li>Security, reliability, and edge cases deserve more attention—not less—as the amount of generated code grows.</li>
          <li>Engineers will spend more time shaping systems and validating outcomes, and less time typing every line by hand.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="disagreement">
          <h3>One friendly disagreement: how much is “more”?</h3>
          <p><strong>Vaibhav's side:</strong> AI may eventually automate software engineering almost entirely, turning a raw idea into working software with little human intervention.</p>
          <p><strong>The cautious side:</strong> The rest of us expect engineers to stay in the loop as architects, testers, and security auditors—because “it compiled” is not the same as “it is safe.”</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <p>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. © <span id="year">2026</span> · Patch Wizards</p>
  </footer>

  <script>
    (function () {
      const root = document.documentElement;
      const toggle = document.getElementById("theme-toggle");
      const icon = toggle.querySelector("[aria-hidden='true']");
      const label = toggle.querySelector(".toggle-label");

      function updateToggle(theme) {
        const next = theme === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
        icon.textContent = theme === "dark" ? "☀️" : "🌙";
        label.textContent = theme === "dark" ? "Day mode" : "Night mode";
        toggle.setAttribute("aria-label", "Switch to " + next + " mode");
      }

      updateToggle(root.getAttribute("data-theme") || "light");

      toggle.addEventListener("click", function () {
        const next = root.getAttribute("data-theme") === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
        root.setAttribute("data-theme", next);
        updateToggle(next);
        try {
          localStorage.setItem("upb-theme", next);
        } catch (_) {
          /* Theme still changes for this visit if storage is unavailable. */
        }
      });

      document.getElementById("year").textContent = new Date().getFullYear();
    }());
  </script>
</body>
</html>
